{
  "id": "Compound::DB00657 - Gene::1143",
  "verbalization": "Mecamylamine appears to interact with multiple subtypes of acetylcholine receptors, specifically Chrna2 and Chrna3, which are involved in the regulation of acetylcholine receptor activity. These receptors are part of the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor family, and their participation in the activity of Chrnb4 suggests a potential functional link between Mecamylamine and the protein Chrnb4. The evidence indicates that Mecamylamine binds to these receptors, which in turn are involved in the activation of Chrnb4, implying a possible role in modulating the activity of Chrnb4 through receptor-mediated signaling. The integration of these pathways highlights the involvement of Mecamylamine in the regulation of acetylcholine receptor function, which may have implications for the biological processes governed by Chrnb4. The relationship is supported by the shared participation of Chrna2 and Chrna3 in the activation of Chrnb4, suggesting a mechanism where Mecamylamine influences the activity of Chrnb4 via these receptor subtypes.",
